# DESCRIPTION

linlon drives an Arm China Linlon-D6 display engine, which is Arm's Mali-D71 design, and serves the display class (`todhchai.display.device`'s `Display`, version 2) by flipping only.
The output firmware lit at boot keeps its mode, its link and its DisplayPort transmitter, and only the buffer its layer scans out changes.
devmgr(8) starts one for each engine, five on the CIX Sky1, and mounts its tree at `dev/display<n>`:

```text
driver linlon
class display
match acpi CIXH5010
```

An engine that isn't powered stops the machine on its first register read, so linlon first reads `/dev/acpi/power`, which says what the engine's power resources' `_STA` say until a driver writes it (devmgr(8)).
If they are off, firmware didn't light this engine. linlon then reads no register, and its display has no outputs.

Otherwise it probes the engine:
- `CORE_ID` must name a part with the D71's registers: a Linlon-D6 (product `0x0060`), Mali-D71 or Mali-D32;
- the global control unit must be in an active mode, which gives the pipeline that scans out;
- that pipeline's enabled layer gives the buffer, its stride and its format, which linlon saves;
- the timing controller gives the mode;
- the refresh is measured over 30 frames of the output unit's once-a-frame interrupt, which is then masked again.

The display's one output, 0, is the layer's size.
It has no EDID: the Sky1's HDMI comes through a PS185 converter that gives none.

`attach` maps a client's image to be read.
`show` of an image the output's size makes two scan-out buffers of linlon's own, the first time, and shows the image.
`flush` copies into the buffer not being scanned out the damage, plus what the last flush put only in the other buffer.
It then points the layer at that buffer as XRGB8888, sets the pipeline's configuration-valid bit, and waits for the configuration to latch at the next frame: the GCU's interrupt, or its raw status after 100 ms.
So a flush returns once its frame is the one being scanned out, and nothing tears.
A flush of an image that isn't shown does nothing.
`show` of image 0 puts back firmware's buffer, its stride and its format.

The buffers are contiguous and mapped write-combining: the Sky1's engines don't snoop the CPU's caches (`_CCA` 0, devmgr's `--noncoherent`).

# FILES

The tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/linlon-acpi-_SB_.DPU<n>`, and mounted at `dev/display<n>`.

: `display`
  The display class's service.
: `status`
  Read only.
  Either `off: ...`, where the engine is unpowered and was left alone, or the probe's lines: `powered by firmware`; `Linlon-D6 r<n>p<n>, ARCH_ID <hex>, <n> pipelines, <n> blocks`; `GCU: output 0 active (status <hex>)`; `pipeline <n>, layer <n>: <w>x<h> <format>, stride <n>, at <address>`; `timing <w>x<h>, total <w>x<h> (h <front>/<sync>/<back>, v ...)`; `refresh <hz> Hz over <n> frames`; and `ready: output 0 <w>x<h>`.
  Where the probe stopped, its last line is `idle: firmware left it not scanning out` or `FAILED: <why>`.
  Once ready, two more lines: `images <n>, showing firmware's buffer` (or `image <id>`), and `flips <n>, latched <n>, missed interrupts <n>, timeouts <n>`, where a missed interrupt is a latch seen in the raw status at the deadline.

